# Payout structures

How the simulator pays every possible outcome of a hand. Every number here
comes from `blackjack/engine.py` (`start_hand`, `take_insurance`, `act`,
`_split` and `_settle`) using the default `Rules` in `blackjack/rules.py`.

Owner: Clark (Engine: rules and tests)


## 1. Three words to know

- Bet (stake): the money you put on the hand. It is taken out of the
  bankroll as soon as the hand starts, before any cards are dealt.
- Payout: the money the engine gives back to the bankroll when the hand
  settles. It includes your stake if you get it back.
- Net: your profit or loss on the hand. Net = payout - bet.

Because the stake is deducted first, a win "returns 2x" but only "profits 1x".
Casino odds such as 3:2 or 1:1 always describe the net, not the payout.


## 2. Main hand outcomes (example bet: $10)

| Outcome             | Odds | Payout returned | Net   | Example payout | Example net |
| ------------------- | ---- | --------------- | ----- | -------------- | ----------- |
| Blackjack (natural) | 3:2  | bet x 2.5       | +1.5x | $25            | +$15        |
| Win                 | 1:1  | bet x 2         | +1x   | $20            | +$10        |
| Push (tie)          | -    | bet x 1         | 0     | $10            | $0          |
| Loss                | -    | 0               | -1x   | $0             | -$10        |
| Bust                | -    | 0               | -1x   | $0             | -$10        |
| Surrender           | -    | bet x 0.5       | -0.5x | $5             | -$5         |

The formulas in `_settle`:

    blackjack:  payout = bet * (1 + rules.blackjack_payout)   # 1 + 1.5
    win:        payout = bet * 2
    push:       payout = bet
    loss/bust:  payout = 0
    surrender:  payout = bet / 2


## 3. What counts as each outcome

- Blackjack: exactly two cards totalling 21 on the original hand
  (an ace plus a ten-value card). See `Hand.is_blackjack`.
- Win: the dealer busts, or your total is higher than the dealer's.
- Push: same total as the dealer. Your stake comes back.
- Loss: your total is lower than the dealer's.
- Bust: your total goes over 21. You lose right away, even if the dealer
  busts later, because the player always acts first.
- Surrender: you give up after seeing your first two cards and get half
  your bet back. Late surrender only: two-card original hand, not after a
  split, and only after the dealer has checked for blackjack.


## 4. The order the engine checks outcomes

`_settle` checks each hand in this exact order and stops at the first match.
The order matters, so a surrendered hand is never treated as a loss, and a
bust is never paid just because the dealer also busted.

1. Surrendered?            -> return half the bet
2. Dealer has blackjack?   -> push if you also have blackjack, otherwise loss
3. You have blackjack?     -> pay 3:2
4. You busted?             -> loss
5. Dealer busted or you are higher? -> win 1:1
6. You are lower?          -> loss
7. Otherwise (equal)       -> push


## 5. Doubles

Doubling adds a second bet the same size as the first, then you take exactly
one more card. The hand's bet becomes 2x, and every outcome above is paid on
the doubled bet.

| Doubled $10 hand (total bet $20) | Payout | Net   |
| -------------------------------- | ------ | ----- |
| Win                              | $40    | +$20  |
| Push                             | $20    | $0    |
| Loss or bust                     | $0     | -$20  |

Allowed on any first two cards, and after a split (double after split is on).


## 6. Splits

Splitting a pair makes two separate hands. The second hand gets a new bet
equal to the first, so a $10 split puts $20 at risk.

- Each hand settles on its own and writes its own `HandResult`, so one hand
  can win while the other loses.
  Example: hand A wins (+$10), hand B loses (-$10), round net = $0.
- 21 after a split is NOT a blackjack. An ace plus a ten on a split hand is a
  normal 21 and pays 1:1, not 3:2.
- Split aces get one card each and cannot be hit. Re-splitting aces is off.
- Up to 4 hands per round from splits.
- You cannot surrender a split hand.


## 7. Dealer blackjack (the peek)

American rules: when the dealer shows an ace or a ten-value card, they check
for blackjack before you act. If they have it, the round ends at once.

- You lose only your original bet (you never got the chance to double or split).
- If you also have blackjack, it is a push and you keep your bet.

| $10 bet, dealer has blackjack | Payout | Net   |
| ----------------------------- | ------ | ----- |
| You do not have blackjack     | $0     | -$10  |
| You also have blackjack       | $10    | $0    |


## 8. Insurance (side bet)

Offered only when the dealer's up card is an ace. Insurance is a separate bet
of half your main bet that pays 2:1 if the dealer has blackjack.

    insurance bet = bet / 2
    dealer has blackjack:  insurance payout = insurance bet * 3   (stake + 2:1)
    dealer has no blackjack: insurance bet is lost

| $10 main bet, $5 insurance          | Insurance net | Main hand net   | Round net   |
| ----------------------------------- | ------------- | --------------- | ----------- |
| Dealer blackjack, you don't         | +$10          | -$10            | $0          |
| Dealer blackjack, you do            | +$10          | $0 (push)       | +$10        |
| No dealer blackjack, you have BJ    | -$5           | +$15            | +$10        |
| No dealer blackjack, hand plays on  | -$5           | played normally | -$5 + hand  |

Notice the first row: insurance exactly cancels the loss, which is why it is
called "protecting" the hand. Rows two and three show "even money": with a
blackjack plus insurance you net +$10 (1:1) whether or not the dealer has
blackjack. It is still a bad bet for a basic-strategy
player, because the dealer has blackjack less than one third of the time,
while 2:1 odds only break even at exactly one third.

Note for the code: insurance is credited straight to the bankroll in
`take_insurance`. It is not part of `HandResult.net`.


## 9. Full summary (bet = B)

| Situation                      | Net        |
| ------------------------------ | ---------- |
| Blackjack                      | +1.5B      |
| Win                            | +B         |
| Push                           | 0          |
| Loss / bust                    | -B         |
| Surrender                      | -0.5B      |
| Doubled win                    | +2B        |
| Doubled loss                   | -2B        |
| Each split hand                | +B, 0 or -B (or 2B if doubled) |
| 21 on a split hand             | +B (not 3:2) |
| Dealer blackjack, you don't    | -B         |
| Dealer blackjack, you do       | 0          |
| Insurance wins                 | +B (on the B/2 side bet) |
| Insurance loses                | -0.5B      |


## 10. Changing the payouts

Payouts are controlled by `Rules`:

- `blackjack_payout = 1.5` gives 3:2. Set it to `1.2` for a 6:5 table: a $10
  blackjack would then net +$12 instead of +$15. Allowed range is (0, 2].
- `late_surrender = False` removes surrender.
- `insurance_enabled = False` removes insurance.
- `double_after_split = False` blocks doubling on split hands.
- Dealer hitting soft 17 (`--hit-soft-17`) changes how often you win, not
  how much a win pays.


## 11. Why this matters for the house edge

Every doubled, split and insurance dollar is added to `total_wagered`.
The house edge is:

    house edge = -sum(net) / sum(wagered)

If doubles and splits were left out of the bottom number, the edge would look
bigger than it really is. Getting each payout right is what makes the
simulation's house edge (about 0.35% with flat basic strategy) trustworthy.
